14150 0 obj
<</A 28073 0 R /Alt(Figure 10.8: Memory chip constructed using two-level decoding. Buffer enable signals shown in green. There are two 2 to 4 decoders. The upper decoder has inputs A3 and A2 and enables tri-state buffers connected to the outputs of flip-flops. The O0 output of the decoder enables flip-flops  labeled 01, 00, 11, 10, 21, 20, 31, and 30. The O1 output of the decoder enables flip-flops  labeled 41, 40, 51, 50, 61, 60, 71, and 70. The O2 output of the decoder enables flip-flops  labeled 81, 80, 91, 90, 101, 100, 111, and 110. The O3 output of the decoder enables flip-flops  labeled 121, 120, 131, 130, 141, 140, 151, and 150. The lower decoder has A1 and A0 as its inputs. the outputs of 01, 41, 81, and 121 are connected to the input of a tri-state buffer that is enabled by output O0 of the lower decoder. This output also enables a tri-state buffer with its input connected to the output of 00, 40, 80, and 120. Decoder output O1 enables buffers connected to the outputs of 1i, 5i, 9i, and 13i. Decoder output O2 enables buffers connected to the outputs of 2i, 6i, 10i, and 14i. Decoder output O3 enables buffers connected to the outputs of 3i, 7i, 11i, and 15i. The tri-state buffers with bits Xi as their inputs are connected to output D1, and the tri-state buffers with bits X0 as their inputs are connected to output D0.␀)/K 8/P 39 0 R /Pg 18713 0 R /S/Figure/T()>>
endobj
